const analyticsSyllabi = {
  'Statistics and Mathematics for Data Science': r'''
Develop quantitative foundations for analysis, experiments and predictive models. | Explain probability and uncertainty. | Apply linear algebra and calculus. | Review statistical evidence for business decisions. | School algebra and basic Python. | Python 3, statistics, NumPy | Analyze an experiment with interval estimates, multiple-test controls and an actionable decision memo.
# Descriptive statistics | Summarize center, spread and shape. | Compare two service queues with robust summaries and labeled plots.
Center and skew | The mean is sensitive to extreme observations, while the median depends on position in sorted data. Report both when skew makes typical experience differ from average cost. | Mean; Median; Right skew | from statistics import mean,median\nx=[2,3,3,4,38]\nprint(mean(x),median(x)) | 10 and 3 | Service teams distinguish typical waits from costly tail cases. | Replace 38 with 100 and explain how both summaries change.
Variability and units | Sample variance divides squared deviations by n minus one. Standard deviation takes its square root and returns to the measurement unit. | Sample variance; Standard deviation; Units | from statistics import variance,stdev\nprint(variance([2,4,6]),stdev([2,4,6])) | 4 and 2 | Quality engineers compare machine consistency. | Multiply observations by three and explain both scaling factors.
? Which central summary resists one very large outlier? | Median | Mean | Sum | Its value depends on the middle ordered position.
? Variance is 9 square seconds. What is standard deviation? | 3 seconds | 9 seconds | 81 seconds | Take the square root and return to seconds.
# Probability and evidence | Use conditional populations and base rates. | Build a table for a rare-event alert and calculate precision and sensitivity.
Conditional probability | P(A given B) uses cases in B as its denominator. Reversing conditions changes the population and usually the result. | Conditional probability; Denominator; Contingency table | flagged,real_flagged=100,12\nprint(real_flagged/flagged) | 0.12 | Fraud reviews estimate how many alerts are genuine. | Calculate sensitivity if there are 20 real fraud cases overall.
Bayesian updates | Posterior probability combines prevalence with likelihood under each condition. For rare events, false positives may dominate despite high sensitivity. | Prior; Bayes rule; False positive | p=.01;s=.9;f=.05\nprint(round(p*s/(p*s+(1-p)*f),3)) | 0.154 | Trust teams account for real incidence when setting alert policy. | Compare a prevalence of 10% and explain the change.
? The denominator of P(fraud given flag) is which group? | Flagged transactions | Fraudulent transactions | All transactions | Conditioning limits the population to flagged cases.
? Why can a sensitive rare-event detector have low precision? | False alarms accumulate among many negatives | Sensitivity equals precision | Positive labels prove fraud | Base rate and specificity both affect a positive result's credibility.
# Distributions and sampling | Match models to assumptions and quantify variation. | Simulate trial counts and compare estimated with expected values.
Binomial counts | A binomial assumes fixed independent Bernoulli trials with a common success probability. Check whether repeated customers or varying exposure violate these assumptions. | Bernoulli; Independence; Binomial | from math import comb\nprint(round(comb(5,2)*.2**2*.8**3,3)) | 0.205 | Conversion analysis models successes under explicit trial assumptions. | Compare probabilities of zero and one conversion.
Standard error | For independent observations, a mean's standard error scales as standard deviation divided by square root of sample size. Dependence can make this formula optimistic. | Standard error; Sampling distribution; Independence | from math import sqrt\nprint(12/sqrt(36),12/sqrt(144)) | 2.0 and 1.0 | Audit planning compares precision at alternative sample sizes. | Find n to reduce standard error to 0.5 when standard deviation is 12.
? What is required by a binomial model? | Fixed trials, independence and constant probability | Continuous outcomes only | Probability changes every trial | These assumptions define the binomial trial process.
? Quadrupling independent sample size changes standard error how? | Halves it | Quarters it | Doubles it | Square root of the sample size doubles.
# Intervals and tests | Interpret uncertainty without overstating evidence. | Report effect size, interval and a prespecified test decision.
Confidence intervals | A normal-approximation interval is estimate plus or minus a multiplier times standard error. Coverage describes the long-run procedure, not a probability assigned to a fixed parameter after observation. | Estimate; Standard error; Coverage | e,se=50,2\nprint(e-1.96*se,e+1.96*se) | Approximately 46.08 to 53.92 | Workforce planning quantifies estimated handling-time uncertainty. | Double the standard error and compare interval widths.
P-values | A p-value measures tail extremeness under the null model and assumptions. Pair it with effect size and uncertainty rather than interpreting it as the probability the null is true. | Null; Alpha; Effect size | p,alpha=.03,.05\nprint('reject' if p<alpha else 'do not reject') | reject under the stated rule | Experiment reviewers apply pre-agreed decision criteria. | Write a careful conclusion for p=0.08 without claiming equality.
? What happens to a fixed-level interval when standard error falls? | It narrows | It widens | Its center becomes zero | Half-width is proportional to standard error.
? What does a p-value describe? | Tail extremeness under the null assumptions | Probability the null is true | Practical effect size | It is a conditional measure under a hypothesized model.
# Linear algebra | Track shapes in feature scoring and transformations. | Score a small batch with documented dimensions.
Dot products | Multiply aligned components and sum to produce a linear score. Inputs must have matching feature meaning and dimension; equal length alone does not ensure semantic alignment. | Vector; Dot product; Weights | x,w=[2,5],[3,-1]\nprint(sum(a*b for a,b in zip(x,w))) | 1 | Risk scores sum weighted customer features. | Add a bias and reorder features to demonstrate a schema error.
Matrix multiplication | An m by n matrix times n by p yields m by p. Each output is a row-column dot product, enabling batch scoring. | Shape; Inner dimension; Batch | import numpy as np\nprint(np.array([[2,5],[1,4]]) @ np.array([3,-1])) | [1 -1] | Inference systems score many records at once. | Add a third record and predict the output dimensions.
? Dot product of [2,5] and [3,-1] is what? | 1 | 11 | -4 | The products 6 and -5 sum to 1.
? A 5×3 matrix times a 3×2 matrix has what shape? | 5×2 | 3×3 | 2×5 | Inner dimensions contract and outer dimensions remain.
# Calculus and optimization | Use derivatives to minimize a loss. | Compare a stable and unstable gradient-descent step size.
Loss derivatives | For squared error (w-target)^2, the derivative is 2(w-target). Its sign indicates which direction increases the loss locally. | Derivative; Slope; Squared loss | w,target=5,2\nprint(2*(w-target)) | 6 | Forecast fitting adjusts coefficients from error gradients. | Calculate the derivative at w=-1 and predict descent direction.
Gradient steps | Subtract learning rate times the gradient. Track loss because a large step can overshoot, oscillate or diverge even for a simple convex loss. | Gradient; Learning rate; Convergence | w=5.0\nfor _ in range(3):w-=.25*2*(w-2)\nprint(w) | 2.375 | Model training diagnoses unstable optimization before deployment. | Repeat with rate 1.2 and compare three losses.
? Derivative of (w−2)^2 at w=5 is what? | 6 | 9 | -6 | The slope is twice the prediction error.
? Why subtract the gradient to minimize loss? | It points toward local increase | It is always negative | It removes the need for step sizes | The negative gradient is a local descent direction.
# Relationships and regression | Interpret associations and residual patterns. | Fit a trend and diagnose a segment with systematic underprediction.
Correlation limits | Pearson correlation quantifies linear association. Confounding, reverse causation and selection can create strong associations without a direct causal effect. | Pearson; Confounding; Nonlinearity | from statistics import correlation\nprint(correlation([1,2,3],[2,4,6])) | 1.0 | Seasonal demand can move spend and sales together. | Construct a nonlinear relationship whose linear correlation is near zero.
Residuals | Residuals equal observed minus predicted values. Patterns by time, magnitude or subgroup can reveal bias, misspecification or changing variability. | Residual; Bias; Diagnostics | a=[11,14,20];p=[10,15,18]\nprint([x-y for x,y in zip(a,p)]) | [1, -1, 2] | Retail forecasting finds underprediction in high-volume stores. | Calculate group mean residuals and identify the more biased group.
? A correlation of 1 proves what? | Perfect positive linear association in the sample | A causal relationship | Identical units | Correlation alone does not establish causality.
? Actual 20 minus predicted 18 gives what residual? | +2 | -2 | 38 | Residual is observed minus predicted.
# Experiment review | Control multiple comparisons and plan meaningful tests. | Critique an underpowered experiment and propose a better design.
Multiple testing | Repeated hypotheses increase the chance of at least one false rejection. Bonferroni divides a family-wise threshold over the prespecified family. | Family-wise error; Bonferroni; Prespecification | print(.05/5) | 0.01 per test | Product reviews protect against false wins among many metrics. | Review p-values .008, .02 and .04 under that cutoff.
Power planning | Power is the chance of rejecting the null under a particular true effect. Planning requires meaningful effect, variance, sample size and significance level. | Power; Minimum effect; Variability | Pilot detects 5 pp lift; business needs to distinguish 1 pp. | The pilot may lack sensitivity for the decision. | Teams avoid interpreting a small inconclusive experiment as proof of no value. | Explain why higher outcome variance increases required exposure.
? Five tests share family alpha .05. What is each Bonferroni cutoff? | .01 | .05 | .25 | Divide alpha by five.
? Why set a minimum meaningful effect before collection? | It ties sample planning to the decision | It changes historical outcomes | It eliminates uncertainty | Power and exposure requirements depend on the target effect.
''',
  'Data Science': r'''
Deliver an end-to-end reproducible investigation from business question through validated analysis and operational handoff. | Define a decision and eligible population. | Audit, explore and analyze data. | Communicate uncertainty and monitor delivered insights. | Basic Python; statistics course recommended. | Python 3, pandas, Jupyter | Investigate subscription retention with data contracts, cohort analysis, model evaluation and a decision memo.
# Framing decisions | Define scope, metrics and guardrails. | Write a brief with decision owner, observation window and exclusions.
Population definitions | Freeze eligibility and the observation window before calculating a rate. A changed denominator can create an apparent improvement without any changed customer outcome. | Population; Cohort; Denominator | eligible,renewed=80,60\nprint(renewed/eligible) | 0.75 | Subscription teams measure renewal on accounts eligible at month start. | Decide whether ten midmonth acquisitions belong in this denominator.
Guardrails | A guardrail is a pre-agreed limit on an unwanted consequence. Measure it alongside the primary metric so optimizing the headline cannot conceal harm. | Baseline; Guardrail; Percentage points | Renewals 75% to 79%; complaints 2% to 5%; complaint ceiling 3%. | Primary metric improves but guardrail fails. | Product teams weigh conversion gains against service complaints. | Design a quality guardrail for a faster review queue.
? Which denominator fits renewal among accounts eligible at month start? | That starting eligible cohort | All sessions | Next month's acquisitions | The measurement population is fixed by the question.
? What should happen when a pre-agreed complaint limit is breached? | Investigate before rollout | Ignore complaints | Redefine the limit after results | The guardrail constrains the rollout decision.
# Data contracts | Audit types, keys, ranges and capture gaps. | Publish a data dictionary and a quarantined-record report.
Validation rules | Define allowed types and ranges, then quarantine invalid records with reasons. Domain exceptions such as refunds need explicit event types rather than blanket deletion. | Schema; Range; Quarantine | rows=[('A',4),('B',-1)]\nprint([k for k,n in rows if n<0]) | ['B'] | Payment data distinguishes invalid charges from legitimate refund records. | Extend the rule with an event type and accepted-value table.
Missingness slices | Compare absence by source or subgroup before imputing. Global averages can hide systematic collection failures and population differences. | Missingness; Slice; Bias | x={'web':[1,None,2],'store':[None,None,4]}\nprint({k:sum(v is None for v in a)/len(a) for k,a in x.items()}) | web about .333; store about .667 | Retail teams locate store-specific capture problems. | Add a third channel and a minimum sample rule.
? What enforces one row per customer? | Unique customer_id | Sum of customer_id | Sorted names | A uniqueness check protects customer grain.
? Why report missingness by channel? | Averages can hide source-specific failures | Missingness is always random | It fills absent fields | Segmentation reveals collection disparities.
# Exploratory analysis | Compare distributions and properly pooled summaries. | Build a numeric profile and an accompanying labeled chart.
Robust summaries | Medians summarize typical values under skew. Means still answer total-per-record questions but respond strongly to extremes, so explain the purpose of each statistic. | Median; Mean; Skew | from statistics import mean,median\nx=[10,11,12,13,100]\nprint(mean(x),median(x)) | 29.2 and 12 | Service teams separate typical experience and average cost. | Add an extreme low value and compare sensitivity.
Weighted rates | Pool successes and exposures before division. Averaging segment rates weights every segment equally even when their sizes differ sharply. | Weight; Exposure; Aggregation | print((9+18)/(10+90)) | .27 | Marketing combines campaign conversion using visitor counts. | Compare with the simple average of .9 and .2.
? Which summary best represents typical skewed waits? | Median | Maximum | Sum | It resists isolated extreme delays.
? Combined conversions 9/10 and 18/90 give what rate? | 27% | 55% | 108% | Total 27 over 100 eligible observations.
# Features and cohorts | Use historical information and equal exposure windows. | Build one feature snapshot per account and a cohort retention table.
As-of features | Include observations available before prediction, not merely events whose event-time is earlier. Late ingestion can otherwise make offline features unrealistic. | Event time; Availability; Leakage | rows=[(2,5),(4,7),(7,10)];cutoff=5\nprint(sum(v for d,v in rows if d<cutoff)) | 12 | Collections models avoid future payment information. | Add availability timestamps and require both clocks to pass.
Cohort retention | Compare customers at equal tenure and show not-yet-observed periods separately from churn. A young cohort is not evidence of zero future retention. | Cohort age; Censoring; Retention | print(35/50) | .7 at the measured cohort age | SaaS teams compare acquisition cohorts. | Explain why a one-month cohort cannot yet supply month-two retention.
? Which record leaks into a day-five prediction? | Data first available on day seven | A day-two record available then | A fixed region known on day one | Future availability violates the inference boundary.
? How should an immature month-two cohort cell appear? | Not yet observed | Zero | Full retention | It has not received the required exposure.
# Experiments | Separate effect size, uncertainty and causal identification. | Specify assignment unit, primary metric and a meaningful effect.
Lift definitions | Absolute lift subtracts rates; relative lift divides the difference by baseline. State units because percentage points and relative percent answer different questions. | Absolute lift; Relative lift; Baseline | a,b=.10,.12\nprint(round(100*(b-a),1),round(100*(b-a)/a,1)) | 2 percentage points and 20% relative | Checkout teams estimate realistic incremental orders. | Estimate added conversions among 40,000 comparable visits.
Assignment units | Randomize at a stable unit and check spillover. Repeated visits by one person should not independently expose them to competing account-level policies. | Randomization; Spillover; Unit | Assign each customer to one fixed variant and measure one seven-day outcome. | Repeated visits remain in the same exposure group. | Retention tests avoid contamination across visits. | Decide whether households require a broader assignment unit.
? A move from 10% to 12% is what absolute lift? | 2 percentage points | 20 percentage points | 12 percentage points | Subtract .10 from .12.
? Why assign an account-level treatment by account? | Keep repeated visits in one group | Increase distinct customers | Eliminate metric collection | Consistent exposure reduces contamination.
# Predictive analysis | Benchmark and test under realistic future conditions. | Compare a simple predictor with a majority baseline on a time holdout.
Class imbalance | Majority predictions can achieve high accuracy while detecting no positives. Choose recall, precision or a cost function according to the decision. | Accuracy; Recall; Baseline | y=[0,0,0,0,1];p=[0]*5\nprint(sum(a==b for a,b in zip(y,p))/len(y)) | .8 accuracy with zero positive recall | Collections triage evaluates misses explicitly. | Compute precision behavior when there are no positive predictions.
Temporal splits | Train on earlier observations and hold out later periods when deployment forecasts the future. Fit preprocessing on training only. | Chronology; Holdout; Drift | days=list(range(1,11))\nprint(days[:7],days[7:]) | Train 1–7; test 8–10 | Demand models simulate future forecasting. | Add a validation period between training and final test.
? Recall of a never-positive classifier when positives exist is what? | Zero | One | Accuracy | No actual positives are detected.
? Which split matches next-month forecasting? | Earlier months for training, later month for test | Mixed future and past rows | Training rows for test | Chronology reproduces the production prediction boundary.
# Reproducibility | Capture inputs, deterministic samples and testable transformations. | Turn a notebook into a parameterized script with a tiny fixture.
Seeded samples | Fix the random seed and stabilize input ordering. A seed alone does not fix data versions, package behavior or sampling order. | Seed; Snapshot; Stable ordering | import random\nprint(random.Random(7).sample(sorted(['d','a','c','b']),2)) | ['c', 'a'] | Analysts share the exact quality-review sample. | Shuffle input order and show why sorting recovers reproducibility.
Data assertions | Validate contracts before writing outputs and use small fixtures to test failure paths. A successful run is insufficient if invalid rows silently pass. | Assertion; Fixture; Unique key | ids=['a','b','a']\nassert len(ids)==len(set(ids)), 'duplicate customer id' | AssertionError: duplicate customer id | Scheduled analyses stop before publishing corrupted results. | Add explicit checks for missing IDs and empty input.
? What else matters besides a seed when reproducing a sample? | Input ordering and version | Chart color | Analyst name | Sampling acts on a particular ordered input.
? When should duplicate-key assertions run? | Before analysis outputs are published | Only afterward | Only on sorted values | Early checks prevent invalid inputs propagating.
# Communication and operation | Deliver recommendations with scope and owned follow-up. | Publish a decision memo, monitoring thresholds and a recovery owner.
Uncertainty statements | Report population, effect and interval together. Use causal wording only under justified identification assumptions, and identify mismatches to the decision population. | Scope; Interval; Limitations | Uplift estimate 3 pp; interval -1 to +7 pp on 400 eligible accounts. | Evidence includes no effect and positive effects. | Executives weigh pilot uncertainty against rollout costs. | Write a two-sentence recommendation reflecting the interval.
Freshness monitoring | Monitor source age separately from the business metric. A delayed feed can mimic collapse, so label stale outputs and route alerts to an owner. | Freshness; Threshold; Playbook | Warn when an expected daily extract is over 26 hours old; escalate after two misses. | A measurable trigger and responder. | Reporting operations distinguish ingestion problems from real change. | Add business-day and weekend schedules.
? What accompanies an uplift estimate in a memo? | Population, uncertainty and limitations | Only best-case outcome | An unlabeled chart | Decision makers need scope and precision.
? Why monitor source freshness separately? | A stale feed can imitate business decline | Late data always increases conversions | It changes the renewal definition | Input health helps explain apparent anomalies.
''',
